% Pierre Aubry, Trouvères et troubadours (1909).
% Published early from the workshop on 11 November 2026: each tune names what is guessed or left out in its N: lines,
% and its workshop record (or review item) is kept for further correction.

X:1
T:A l'entrada del tens clar
T:At the Coming of the Bright Season
C:anonymous (Occitan, 12th-13th century)
O:Occitania (southern France)
R:balada (round dance)
S:Pierre Aubry, Trouvères et troubadours (Paris: Félix Alcan, 1909), pp. 60-61, from the chansonnier of Saint-Germain-des-Prés (Paris, BnF fr. 20050)
N:A troubadour round dance for the coming of spring: the "April queen" calls every girl and young man to come and dance, to spite the jealous old king. A soloist (or the teacher) sings each line, the class answers "E-ya!", and everyone sings the refrain, "Away, away, jealous ones, let us dance among ourselves", dancing in a ring. The manuscript writes no rhythm: the long-short 3/4 swing, the grace notes and the two F sharps are Aubry's 1909 reading. Guessed: the F sharp on the second "jelos" in the refrain (the head sits high on the plate, but its sharp and the G-F sharp-G cadence want F sharp) and the second "E-ya!" (B, G, A, where the other two go B, c, d), both as the record reads them. Aubry marks it "Vif"; the tempo and the tabor-like drum are ours.
Q:1/4=150
M:3/4
L:1/4
K:Gmix
